module

public import Rule30Open.Columns.WordTraps

/-!
# Traps for 34 more blocks of periods 10 to 12, the closed sets that driven right halves settle into

Driven by a block of period 10, 11 or 12 for 12,000 steps, a right half often ends inside a closed set of windows on
which column 1 is fixed at the white phases: the closure of its window under both values of the cell beyond. The natural
scan of [natural traps](../../findings/columns/natural-traps.md) finds one for 35 blocks that the kit and the finite game
do not exclude and that the walls of [TenTraps](TenTraps.md), [ElevenTraps](ElevenTraps.md) and
[TwelveTraps](TwelveTraps.md) do not carry. Here the kernel checks 34 of them, 3 blocks of period 10, 13 of period 11 and
18 of period 12, with 107 to 15,752 states; the 35th, of 61,857 states for $00010001011$, is left out for its cost.

* `nat<block>`, `nat<block>_check`: the traps, each passing the check of [WordTraps](WordTraps.md).
* `naturalTraps`, `naturalTraps_check`, `naturalTraps_blocks`: the list, its check and its blocks.
* `natural_absorption_excludes`: for each of them, if every finitely supported right half driven by the block enters
  its trap, the center column does not eventually repeat the block.

The tables come from `python -m computations.lock_chain --lean-extra`. The natural-language argument is
`NaturalTraps.md`, beside this file.
-/

@[expose] public section

-- Sequential elaboration: the kernel checks would otherwise run in parallel and stack their memory.
set_option Elab.async false

namespace Rule30Open

open Ruliology.CellularAutomata

